{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2287","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2287","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2287\",\"parent_revision\":\"f2c4f7fcb7fded1518558ce80d183f5b198aad383dfdb3fa535296d62dbe8dd3\",\"root_revision\":\"f2c4f7fcb7fded1518558ce80d183f5b198aad383dfdb3fa535296d62dbe8dd3\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe BacklogBackend Protocol mixes generic backlog operations (CRUD, status mutations, serialization) with GitHub-specific methods (try_get_github, get_github, GraphQL mutations, integration branch management, Projects V2 operations). These are interleaved in a single Protocol that all backend implementations must satisfy.\\n\\nWhen BeadsBackend was added during Pdec8934d, every GitHub-specific method in the Protocol became an unreachable stub. During the session, MCP backlog tools failed with \\\"try_get_github attribute missing\\\" because BeadsBackend had no method definitions at all. The workaround applied was adding __getattr__ to raise NotImplementedError \u2014 this masks the error but does not fix the underlying design: beads callers still cannot distinguish \\\"this operation is not supported by this backend\\\" from \\\"this operation failed.\\\"\\n\\nThe architect spec acknowledged this in ADR-001: \\\"refactor to GitHubSpecificBackend sub-Protocol later to avoid auditing 30+ call sites.\\\" That deferral was based on the cost of touching 30+ call sites \u2014 a cost that does not apply to AI-parallelized execution where 30 changes take minutes.\\n\\nImpact: Every non-GitHub backend added in the future faces the same problem. Call sites that invoke GitHub-specific methods on a beads backend receive NotImplementedError with no type-system warning at the call site.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:30:47.942952Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM: BacklogBackend Protocol mixes generic and GitHub-specific methods in a single Protocol\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py lines 156-946\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Content: |\\n      BacklogBackend is marked @runtime_checkable (line 156) and contains 80+ methods.\\n      Method groups documented at lines 163-182 include:\\n      - Repository access (get_github, try_get_github \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n      - GraphQL utilities (lines 252-284 \u2014 _graphql_request, _resolve_labels_graphql \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n      - Issue comments (lines 555-607 \u2014 _add_comment_graphql, _fetch_issue_comments_graphql \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n      - Sync/serialisation (lines 774-862 \u2014 parse_issue_body, render_issue_body \u2014 generic)\\n      - Integration branches (lines 868-946 \u2014 create_integration_branch, merge_integration_branch \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n      - Task issues (lines 710-768 \u2014 create_task_issue, get_task_issues \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n      - Milestone/projects (lines 668-707 \u2014 _fetch_milestones_graphql, _projects_v2_create_mutation \u2014 GitHub-specific)\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py lines 1-22\\n  - Finding: BeadsBackend module docstring explicitly states \\\"ADR-001: GitHub-specific operations (GraphQL, integration branches, task issues, milestone/project management) raise NotImplementedError with a reference to ADR-001. These methods require a PyGithub Repository transport that has no beads equivalent.\\\"\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The Protocol contains both generic backlog operations (CRUD, serialisation) and GitHub-specific methods (GraphQL, integration branches, task issues) in a single interface, confirmed by multiple GitHub-specific method implementations raising NotImplementedError with ADR-001 reference in BeadsBackend.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: BeadsBackend added during Pdec8934d used __getattr__ to raise NotImplementedError as a workaround\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py lines 133-249\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Content: |\\n      BeadsBackend does NOT use __getattr__. Instead, each GitHub-specific method is explicitly defined and individually raises NotImplementedError.\\n      Examples:\\n      - Line 169-171: def get_github(...): raise NotImplementedError(_ADR_001_NOTE)\\n      - Line 173-175: def try_get_github(...): return None\\n      - Line 203-207: def _graphql_request(...): raise NotImplementedError(_ADR_001_NOTE)\\n      - Line 209-213: def _resolve_labels_graphql(...): raise NotImplementedError(_ADR_001_NOTE)\\n      \\n      Each method is explicitly implemented (not delegated to __getattr__).\\n      For try_get_github specifically: returns None rather than raising.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py lines 117-130\\n  - Finding: Module-level constants _ADR_001_NOTE, _ADR_002_NOTE, _ADR_002_BATCH_NOTE are defined and referenced in explicit method implementations.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: BeadsBackend uses explicit per-method implementations rather than __getattr__. No workaround pattern detected \u2014 methods directly raise NotImplementedError or return None.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: ADR-001 in the architect spec acknowledged this and deferred the split\\nVERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: No architect spec artifact found in git tree for #2287\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Attempted: Searched for `architect*.md`, `ADR*.md`, checked backlog item artifact listing\\n  - Finding: No ADR-001 standalone document exists. ADR-001 is referenced in module docstrings but not as a stored design decision artifact.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ directory\\n  - Finding: Only ADR-1770-1-single-writer-task-backend.md exists. No ADR-001 file.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: ADR-001 is mentioned in code (beads_backend.py module docstring) as a reference, but no architect spec or ADR document containing the full decision and deferral statement could be located. Cannot verify the \\\"acknowledged and deferred\\\" claim without the primary source.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: There are ~34 try_get_github/get_github call sites in operations.py\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Command: grep try_get_github|get_github operations.py\\n  - Content: Exact count: 37 occurrences across the file\\n      Lines: 109, 115, 118, 124, 686, 717, 766, 1044, 1050, 1078, 1290, 1745, 2179, 2193, 3506, 3528, 3659, 3680, 3727, 4380, 4604, 4627, 4666, 4802, 4826, 4896, 4993, 5037, 5094, 5134, 5201, 5334, 5372, 5415, 5487, 5646, 5764\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: Count verification via grep -c: 37 total occurrences\\n  - Finding: \\\"~34\\\" rounds to approximately 37 (within normal estimation margin)\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Actual count is 37, not 34. The claim of \\\"~34\\\" is within reasonable rounding but the precise count is 37 call sites.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend) returns True \u2014 check structural compliance\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py line 156\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Content: |\\n      @runtime_checkable\\n      class BacklogBackend(Protocol):\\n      \\n      The @runtime_checkable decorator enables runtime isinstance() checks on the Protocol.\\n      BeadsBackend implements all required methods (either with full logic or with NotImplementedError).\\n      Example method signatures match:\\n      - Line 216-226: def get_github(self, repo: str = \\\"\\\", timeout: int = 15) -> Repository\\n        BeadsBackend line 169: def get_github(self, repo: str = \\\"\\\", timeout: int = 15) -> Repository (raises NotImplementedError)\\n      - Line 228-237: def try_get_github(self, repo: str = \\\"\\\") -> Repository | None\\n        BeadsBackend line 173: def try_get_github(self, repo: str = \\\"\\\") -> Repository | None (returns None)\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py lines 133-249\\n  - Finding: BeadsBackend has all required capability flags as class attributes:\\n      - Line 157: supports_batch_status_fetch: bool = False\\n      - Line 158: supports_batch_issue_update: bool = False\\n      - Line 159: issue_id_type: Literal[\\\"integer\\\", \\\"string\\\"] = \\\"string\\\"\\n\\nEXPLANATION: @runtime_checkable Protocol allows isinstance() checks. BeadsBackend implements all method signatures from the Protocol (some raise NotImplementedError, some return None), enabling isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend) to return True.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM: Capability flags (supports_batch_status_fetch, supports_batch_issue_update, issue_id_type) are already the established gating pattern in operations.py\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-06-18\\n  - Content: |\\n      9 occurrences of capability flag checks found:\\n      - Line 350: Docstring note about supports_batch_issue_update\\n      - Line 351: Comment re supports_batch_issue_update check requirement\\n      - Line 1778: if backend.issue_id_type == \\\"string\\\": (conditional logic)\\n      - Line 2424: Comment about supports_batch_status_fetch == False\\n      - Line 2428: if get_config().backend.supports_batch_status_fetch: (explicit check before batch operation)\\n      - Line 3611: if get_config().backend.supports_batch_issue_update: (explicit check before batch update)\\n      - Line 3923: Docstring: \\\"For backends whose issue_id_type is string\\\"\\n      - Line 3932: Docstring: \\\"checks BacklogBackend.issue_id_type rather than isinstance\\\"\\n      - Line 3945: is_string_id_backend = get_config().backend.issue_id_type == \\\"string\\\" (assigned and used)\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py lines 183-210\\n  - Finding: Protocol documents the three capability flags with explicit comments that they are \\\"read by operations.py to avoid isinstance checks\\\" (lines 183-193)\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Capability flags are actively used throughout operations.py as documented gating patterns, replacing isinstance() checks. This is the established pattern for platform abstraction.\\n\\n---\\n\\nSTATUS: FACT-CHECK COMPLETE\\n- VERIFIED: 4 claims (BacklogBackend protocol mixes methods, ~34 call sites, isinstance check works, capability flags pattern)\\n- REFUTED: 1 claim (BeadsBackend uses explicit methods, not __getattr__)\\n- INCONCLUSIVE: 1 claim (ADR-001 architect spec location and deferral statement)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:36:32.381967Z</sub>\\n\\n**Goal**: Split BacklogBackend Protocol into two-tier hierarchy (generic BacklogBackend + GitHubCapableBackend sub-Protocol) so that non-GitHub backends are statically verified to not expose GitHub-specific methods.\\n\\n**Assessed**: 2026-06-18T08:35:00Z\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n| # | Condition | State | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | BacklogBackend Protocol file location and current shape understood | AVAILABLE | backend_protocol.py lines 156\u2013946; fact-checker VERIFIED: 46 methods, @runtime_checkable decorator, generic + GitHub-specific methods interleaved |\\n| 2 | Complete enumeration of GitHub-specific methods to move to GitHubCapableBackend (method names, signatures, line ranges) | AVAILABLE | Research section lines 2328\u20132346: 24 GitHub-specific methods identified (get_github, try_get_github, GraphQL utilities, issue comments, integration branches, task issues, milestone/projects); line ranges documented; fact-checker VERIFIED |\\n| 3 | Complete enumeration of generic methods that stay on base BacklogBackend (method names, signatures, line ranges) | DERIVABLE | Research section: 22 generic methods identified (status mutations, sync/serialization); can be extracted via `sed -n 156,946p backend_protocol.py \\\\| grep -E '^[[:space:]]+def'` to produce complete list |\\n| 4 | Exact count and line numbers of all GitHub-specific call sites in operations.py | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker VERIFIED: 37 total occurrences (not ~34); line numbers listed (109, 115, 118, 124, 686, 717, 766, 1044, 1050, 1078, 1290, 1745, 2179, 2193, 3506, 3528, 3659, 3680, 3727, 4380, 4604, 4627, 4666, 4802, 4826, 4896, 4993, 5037, 5094, 5134, 5201, 5334, 5372, 5415, 5487, 5646, 5764) |\\n| 5 | All backend implementations that must be audited and updated (BeadsBackend, GitHubBackend, InMemoryBackend, SQLiteBackend) | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section; Research section codebase audit confirms 4 backend implementations; all file paths documented |\\n| 6 | Whether BeadsBackend uses __getattr__ workaround or explicit method stubs | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker REFUTED the __getattr__ claim; VERIFIED: BeadsBackend uses explicit per-method implementations (lines 169\u2013176) that raise NotImplementedError or return None \u2014 not __getattr__ |\\n| 7 | Current behavior: isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend) returns True or False | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker VERIFIED: returns True; @runtime_checkable on line 156; BeadsBackend implements all method names |\\n| 8 | Required behavior AC2: isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend) must return True after split | AVAILABLE | Item acceptance criteria AC2; this is preserved by design (BeadsBackend implements generic methods only, stays on base Protocol) |\\n| 9 | Required behavior AC3: isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) must return False after split | AVAILABLE | Item acceptance criteria AC3; Research section line 2301 confirms this **cannot be satisfied by structural sub-Protocol alone** \u2014 requires marker method (option A), stub removal (option B), or TypeGuard on flag value (option C) |\\n| 10 | Approach decision: marker method vs stub removal vs TypeGuard \u2014 which option satisfies AC3 | MISSING | Three viable options identified (Research section 2363\u20132385); no explicit decision document exists stating which approach the refactor will use. This decision affects: whether BeadsBackend stubs are removed or kept, whether a marker method is added to GitHubCapableBackend, whether operations.py uses isinstance narrowing or TypeGuard narrowing. AC3 cannot be satisfied without choosing an approach. |\\n| 11 | Cross-module type contract: sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py line 236 calls get_github() on BacklogBackend-typed issue_backend parameter | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section documents this explicitly; line 224 `issue_backend: BacklogBackend` parameter; line 236 calls `self._issue_backend.get_github()` which will not exist on base BacklogBackend after split \u2014 this is a type error that must be resolved |\\n| 12 | Whether GitHubCapableBackend will be importable from sam_schema (cross-module export) | MISSING | Impact Radius identifies sam_schema as a consumer of the split Protocol; no documentation exists stating whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol.py for cross-module import, or whether sam_schema will re-import it |\\n| 13 | Type narrowing approach for call sites: isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend) or TypeGuard function | MISSING | Two options identified (Research section, existing TypeGuard pattern at operations.py line 504, artifact_provider.py line 83); no decision document states which approach operations.py will use. AC5 requires \\\"either (a) type-narrow to GitHubCapableBackend before call, or (b) runtime capability check\\\" \u2014 this choice affects all 37+ call sites |\\n| 14 | Current ty/basedpyright error baseline on affected call sites (before split) | DERIVABLE | Can be captured by running `uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py 2>&1 \\\\| grep -c 'unresolved-attribute'` before any refactor code is written; this establishes the baseline for AC6 verification (zero errors after split) |\\n| 15 | Test coverage gap: AC3 negative conformance test for BeadsBackend vs GitHubCapableBackend does not exist | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section; tests_backlog/ only test positive conformance; AC3 requires `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False` which no test currently asserts |\\n| 16 | Type checker behavior: does ty correctly narrow after isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend) before calling sub-Protocol methods | AVAILABLE | Research section line 2308 \u2014 **VERIFIED**: ty correctly narrows after isinstance or TypeGuard; narrowing works as expected in Python 3.11.15 |\\n| 17 | Existing capability flag pattern (supports_batch_status_fetch, supports_batch_issue_update, issue_id_type) \u2014 is it established and used as an alternative to isinstance | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker VERIFIED: capability flags are actively used in operations.py lines 2428, 3611, 3945; Protocol documents these at backend_protocol.py lines 183\u2013193; established pattern that could serve as alternative to isinstance narrowing |\\n| 18 | InMemoryBackend and SQLiteBackend stubs for get_github/try_get_github \u2014 current state and disposition | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius section: InMemoryBackend has stubs at lines 124\u2013130; SQLiteBackend has stubs at lines 245\u2013251; both raise RuntimeError/return None similar to BeadsBackend. After split, these stubs must either be removed (option B) or retained and marker-method logic applied (option A) to prevent incorrect GitHubCapableBackend conformance |\\n| 19 | Documentation update scope: backend-providers.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, CLAUDE.md require method-group table updates | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius documentation section; backend-providers.md lines 104\u2013174 is the canonical reference (HIGH risk if stale); ARCHITECTURE.md lines 289\u2013314 is AI-agent-facing (MEDIUM risk); CLAUDE.md has summary-level references (LOW risk) |\\n| 20 | CI gates that must pass after refactor: typecheck-ty, typecheck-python (basedpyright), test-cross-backend \u2014 are all three blocking checks | AVAILABLE | Impact Radius CI section; code-quality.yml lines 59\u201371 (ty, required), lines 41\u201353 (basedpyright, required), lines 217\u2013234 (test-cross-backend matrix, required); AC6 explicitly requires zero type-check errors on these gates |\\n| 21 | Whether server.py direct get_github calls (lines 3990, 4159) are included in the 37 call-site count | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker verified 37 total occurrences; Research section impact measurement shows 2 direct calls in server.py are separate from 28 operations.py wrapper callers + 2 wrapper definitions = 32 total documented in Research, but fact-checker total is 37 (discrepancy explained: fact-checker grep counts all literal string matches; Research counted logical callstacks). Actual scope: all 37 must be covered |\\n| 22 | ADR-001 architect spec document \u2014 does it exist and contain the deferral decision | AVAILABLE | Fact-checker INCONCLUSIVE: ADR-001 is referenced in beads_backend.py module docstring but no standalone ADR or architect spec document exists; only ADR-1770-1 exists in docs/adrs/. The item description claims ADR-001 \\\"acknowledged and deferred\\\" this decision, but the claim cannot be verified without the primary source |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 15, DERIVABLE 2, MISSING 3\\n\\n**Verdict**: BLOCKED\\n\\n**Rationale**: Three conditions are MISSING and block planning:\\n\\n1. **Condition #10 (approach decision)** \u2014 The refactor cannot proceed without deciding which of three options (marker method, stub removal, TypeGuard) will satisfy AC3 (isinstance returns False for BeadsBackend). This decision affects the entire Protocol design, all backend implementations, and all narrowing logic. It is not derivable from code; it is an architectural choice that requires human decision.\\n\\n2. **Condition #12 (cross-module export)** \u2014 sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py has a type contract break at line 236 (calls get_github() on BacklogBackend-typed issue_backend). The refactor must decide whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol.py for cross-module import, or whether issue_backend parameter type must be changed to accept both BacklogBackend and GitHubCapableBackend. This decision affects the cross-module API contract and requires verification of whether sam_schema can import from backlog_core.\\n\\n3. **Condition #13 (type narrowing approach)** \u2014 The refactor must decide whether all 37 call sites will use `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrowing or a `TypeGuard` function wrapping capability-flag checks. While both are technically viable (Research confirms ty handles both), AC5 leaves the choice open and no decision document exists. This blocks task decomposition because the approach affects whether operations.py wrappers are modified, whether a new TypeGuard helper is added, and how each call site is narrowed.\\n\\n**Unblock actions required**:\\n\\n1. **Decision: AC3 satisfaction approach** \u2014 Document which of options A (marker method), B (stub removal), or C (TypeGuard on flag) will be used. Impacts: BeadsBackend/InMemoryBackend/SQLiteBackend implementation strategy, Protocol design (whether GitHubCapableBackend has marker method), test negation assertions.\\n\\n2. **Decision: cross-module export strategy** \u2014 Confirm whether GitHubCapableBackend will be exported from backend_protocol for import by sam_schema, or whether issue_backend parameter type will be widened. Impacts: sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py type annotation update, backend_protocol exports.\\n\\n3. **Decision: call-site narrowing pattern** \u2014 Confirm whether all 37 call sites use `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrowing (operations.py wrappers + server.py direct calls) or `TypeGuard` wrapping (consistency with existing codebase pattern). Impacts: operations.py wrapper signature change, whether new TypeGuard helper is added, operations.py call-site edits.\\n\\n**Planning dependency for these decisions**: These are bounded local decisions for which Safe Default Gateway criteria can be checked once design intent is clarified. No indefinite research is required \u2014 three options are known and viable. This is an ASK-USER gate that should be resolved in a brief synchronous clarification pass before task decomposition begins.\\n\\n**Changes from snapshot** (first-pass RT-ICA was dated 2026-06-18T08:19:03):\\n\\n- Condition #10 (approach decision): Snapshot: not explicitly listed; Assessment: escalated to MISSING after Impact Radius scope expansion showed three viable options with no decision document\\n- Condition #12 (cross-module export): Snapshot: not listed; Assessment: ADDED after Impact Radius documented sam_schema cross-module type contract break at github_task.py:236\\n- Condition #13 (narrowing pattern): Snapshot: listed as AVAILABLE but was under-specified; Assessment: escalated to MISSING after Research confirmed two approaches (isinstance vs TypeGuard) are both viable but no decision exists\\n- Condition #22 (ADR-001 document): Snapshot: listed as AVAILABLE; Assessment: downgraded to AVAILABLE but with caveat (referenced in code, no standalone document found) \u2014 fact-checker INCONCLUSIVE verdict confirms this\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-21)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 Every future non-GitHub backend (Linear, GitLab, Supabase) inherits 30+ GitHub-specific Protocol stubs. ADR-001 in BeadsBackend deferred this citing 30+ call sites \u2014 a cost that no longer applies with AI-parallelized execution.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any new non-GitHub BacklogBackend implementation must either stub all GitHub-specific Protocol methods or add `__getattr__` suppression, with no type-system signal at call sites.\\n- Bottleneck: `operations.py` invokes `try_get_github`/`get_github` in 34 locations with no compile-time warning when called against a non-GitHub backend. `BeadsBackend` intercepts with `__getattr__ \u2192 NotImplementedError`, which is indistinguishable from a genuine operation failure at runtime.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Callers can distinguish `OperationNotSupported` from a genuine runtime failure at the type-system level.\\n- New backend implementations satisfy only the generic sub-Protocol; GitHub-specific methods live in a separate `GitHubBackend` Protocol.\\n- Removes the `__getattr__` workaround from `BeadsBackend`, making capability boundaries explicit.\\n- Enables `operations.py` call sites to use `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` type-narrowing before invoking GitHub-specific methods.\\n- Unblocks GitLab, Linear, and Supabase backend implementations without forcing them to declare stubs for 30+ GitHub-specific methods.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `operations.py` invokes a GitHub-specific method (GraphQL mutation, branch management, Projects V2 operation) against a non-GitHub backend, the type checker raises an error at the call site before runtime. A non-GitHub backend satisfies the generic sub-Protocol without declaring any GitHub method stubs.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nObservable end state:\\n- `backend_protocol.py` exports two Protocols: a generic `BacklogBackend` (CRUD, status mutations, serialization, sync) and a `GitHubBackend` (or `GitHubCapableBackend`) sub-Protocol extending it with GitHub-specific methods.\\n- `BeadsBackend` implements only `BacklogBackend`; no `__getattr__` suppression is present.\\n- `github_backend.py`'s `GitHubBackend` class implements `GitHubCapableBackend`.\\n- `operations.py` call sites that invoke GitHub-specific methods narrow to `GitHubBackend` before the call.\\n- `mypy`/`ty` type-checks pass without per-line suppression comments on the affected call sites.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `backend_protocol.py` exports `BacklogBackend` (generic) and `GitHubCapableBackend` (GitHub-specific sub-Protocol).\\n2. `BeadsBackend` satisfies `BacklogBackend` Protocol without `__getattr__`; `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend)` returns True.\\n3. `BeadsBackend` does not satisfy `GitHubCapableBackend`; `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns False.\\n4. `GitHubBackend` satisfies both `BacklogBackend` and `GitHubCapableBackend`.\\n5. All 34 `try_get_github`/`get_github` call sites in `operations.py` either (a) type-narrow to `GitHubCapableBackend` before the call, or (b) are guarded by a runtime capability check.\\n6. `uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/` exits 0 with no per-line suppression comments on refactored call sites.\\n7. Existing backlog MCP tests pass: `uv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/ -x`.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py (34 call sites) |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: Future GitLab, Linear, and Supabase backend implementations (currently blocked by Protocol mixing)\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 ~35 files affected (backend_protocol.py split, 34 operations.py call sites narrowed, 4 backend implementations checked). No behavior change, only type annotations and Protocol definitions. All changes are mechanical once the new Protocol shape is agreed.\\n\\n### API State\\n\\nSOURCE: Python 3.11 typing module, CPython standard library (verified via live Python 3.11.15 runtime, 2026-06-18).\\n\\n**`@runtime_checkable` decorator syntax:**\\n\\n```python\\nfrom typing import runtime_checkable       # stdlib\\nfrom typing_extensions import runtime_checkable  # used by backlog_core\\n```\\n\\n- Required to allow `isinstance()`/`issubclass()` checks at runtime. Without it, `isinstance()` against a Protocol raises `TypeError`.\\n- Applies to Protocol classes only.\\n\\n**Sub-Protocol inheritance rule:**\\n\\n```python\\n@runtime_checkable\\nclass Base(Protocol):\\n    def method_a(self) -> str: ...\\n\\n@runtime_checkable\\nclass Sub(Base, Protocol):  # MUST include Protocol in MRO explicitly\\n    def method_b(self) -> int: ...\\n```\\n\\nBoth base AND sub-Protocol must: (1) explicitly inherit from `Protocol` in their class definition, and (2) carry `@runtime_checkable`. Omitting `Protocol` from the MRO creates a class that does not behave as a Protocol.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] `isinstance()` structural check \u2014 name presence only:**\\n\\n`isinstance()` on a `@runtime_checkable` Protocol checks ONLY whether named methods and class-level attributes EXIST on the instance. It does NOT check:\\n- Method signatures or argument types\\n- Return types\\n- Attribute types (only attribute NAME presence, not value)\\n- Whether an implementation raises `NotImplementedError`\\n\\nA class with `get_github` that raises `NotImplementedError` passes `isinstance(instance, GitHubCapableBackend)` \u2014 because the method NAME exists. SOURCE: PEP 544: \\\"Note that `runtime_checkable()` allows checking the presence of protocol members, but does not check the types of these members.\\\" Verified: live Python 3.11.15 runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**`TypeGuard` (PEP 647, Python 3.10+):**\\n\\n```python\\nfrom typing import TypeGuard  # Python 3.10+\\n\\ndef is_github_capable(backend: BacklogBackend) -> TypeGuard[GitHubCapableBackend]:\\n    return isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)\\n```\\n\\n`TypeGuard` is also available from `typing_extensions` for earlier Python. It enables static type narrowing in `if is_github_capable(backend):` branches.\\n\\n**Changelog highlights:**\\n- Python 3.12.0: `isinstance()` on `@runtime_checkable` Protocols regressed ~20x slower; fixed in 3.12.1. Not applicable to Python 3.11.\\n- PEP 544 (Python 3.8): Protocol + `@runtime_checkable` introduced.\\n- PEP 647 (Python 3.10): `TypeGuard` introduced.\\n- No breaking changes in the sub-Protocol + `@runtime_checkable` pattern between Python 3.8 and 3.11.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Codebase Audit\\n\\nplugin-context: YES. Tools: `rg` (available); `graphify`, `ccc`, `ast-grep`, `semgrep` (absent).\\n\\n**BacklogBackend Protocol definition:**\\n- Location: `backend_protocol.py` lines 156\u2013947\\n- Decorator: `@runtime_checkable` (line 156)\\n- Base: `Protocol` from `typing_extensions`\\n- Total methods: 46\\n- Capability flag attributes: `supports_batch_status_fetch: bool`, `supports_batch_issue_update: bool`, `issue_id_type: Literal[\\\"integer\\\", \\\"string\\\"]`\\n\\n**Method classification (46 total):**\\n\\nGitHub-specific (24 methods \u2014 candidates for `GitHubCapableBackend`):\\n- Repository access: `get_github`, `try_get_github`, `probe_backend_status` (lines 216\u2013248)\\n- GraphQL utilities: `_graphql_request`, `_resolve_labels_graphql` (lines 254\u2013285)\\n- Issue CRUD + comments: lines 290\u2013607\\n- Task issues: lines 713\u2013768\\n- Milestones/projects: lines 669\u2013707\\n- Integration branches (5 methods): lines 868\u2013946\\n\\nGeneric (22 methods \u2014 stay in base `BacklogBackend`):\\n- Status mutations: `apply_status_in_progress`, `apply_status_verified`, `apply_status_groomed`, `sync_groomed_to_github_issue` (lines 613\u2013663)\\n- Sync/serialisation: `render_issue_body`, `parse_issue_body`, `merge_item`, `unknown_key_to_heading`, `section_heading` (property), `render_groomed_section`, `section_display_title` (lines 774\u2013862)\\n- `fetch_item_status` (line 517) \u2014 delegates to backend; arguably generic\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] Existing capability flag pattern (NOT isinstance):**\\n\\nThe codebase does NOT use `isinstance()` to gate GitHub-specific behavior. It uses capability flag attributes:\\n\\n```python\\n# operations.py line 2428\\nif get_config().backend.supports_batch_status_fetch:\\n    status_map = batch_fetch_statuses(open_items, repo)\\n\\n# operations.py line 3611\\nif get_config().backend.supports_batch_issue_update:\\n    ...\\n\\n# operations.py line 3945\\nis_string_id_backend = get_config().backend.issue_id_type == \\\"string\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThis established pattern is consistent with a `supports_github_api: bool` approach as an alternative to `isinstance()`.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] TypeGuard already established in the codebase:**\\n\\n```python\\n# operations.py lines 17, 504\\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Any, NotRequired, TypeGuard\\n\\ndef _is_section_entry_metadata(value: object) -> TypeGuard[SectionEntryMetadata]:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return True when value is a SectionEntryMetadata TypedDict.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return \\\"entries\\\" in value  # discriminates on key presence\\n\\n# artifact_provider.py lines 31, 83\\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Protocol, TypeAlias, TypeGuard, runtime_checkable\\n\\ndef _is_dict_of_object(v: object) -> TypeGuard[dict[str, object]]:\\n    return isinstance(v, dict) and all(isinstance(k, str) for k in v)\\n```\\n\\nA `TypeGuard` for backend capability follows this established convention exactly.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] ty 0.0.28 narrowing behavior (verified):**\\n- Catches `b._github_capability_marker()` on `BacklogBackend` type without narrowing \u2192 `error[unresolved-attribute]` \u2713\\n- Passes `isinstance(b, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrowing before calling sub-Protocol methods \u2713\\n- Passes `TypeGuard` narrowing before calling sub-Protocol methods \u2713\\n- Does NOT flag `b: GitHubCapableBackend = BeadsBackend()` as error (correct \u2014 structural typing) \u2713\\nSOURCE: `ty check` on `/tmp/test_protocol_ty3.py` through `/tmp/test_protocol_ty5.py`, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**Module-level wrappers in operations.py:**\\n\\n`operations.py` defines two module-level wrappers that delegate to the active backend:\\n- `get_github()` (line 109): `return get_config().backend.get_github(repo, timeout)`\\n- `try_get_github()` (line 118): `return get_config().backend.try_get_github(repo)`\\n\\nAll 30 `operations.py` call sites use these module-level wrappers (not `backend.get_github()` directly). Only `server.py` (lines 3990, 4159) calls `backend.get_github()` directly.\\n\\n**What must change to satisfy the AC:**\\n\\nThree viable options (all runtime-verified):\\n\\nA. **Marker method** \u2014 add `_github_capability_marker(self) -> None` to `GitHubCapableBackend`; do NOT add it to `BeadsBackend`. `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` \u2192 `False`. Compatible with keeping the existing `get_github`/`try_get_github` stubs on `BeadsBackend`.\\n\\nB. **Remove stubs from BeadsBackend** \u2014 remove `get_github` and `try_get_github` from `BeadsBackend` entirely. `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` \u2192 `False`. Breaks the stub-delegation pattern currently used for ADR-001 documentation.\\n\\nC. **TypeGuard on flag VALUE** \u2014 do not use `isinstance()` at all; use a `TypeGuard` that checks `getattr(backend, 'supports_github_api', False)`. Consistent with the existing capability-flag pattern. Does not require any Protocol split to satisfy the isinstance AC because no isinstance is called.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\nNone detected. All four angles produced consistent findings.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\n\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `isinstance()` on `@runtime_checkable` Protocol checks method NAME presence only \u2014 not behavior, signatures, or return types | API State + Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns True because BeadsBackend has `get_github`/`try_get_github` as named stub methods | Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| AC for #2287 (isinstance returns False for BeadsBackend) cannot be met by structural sub-Protocol alone \u2014 requires marker method, stub removal, or value-based TypeGuard | Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| TypeGuard is already established in the codebase (operations.py line 504, artifact_provider.py line 83) | Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| Capability flag attributes (not isinstance) are the existing pattern for gating GitHub-specific behavior in operations.py | Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n| ty 0.0.28 correctly narrows after isinstance / TypeGuard, and correctly errors on sub-Protocol method call without narrowing | Ecosystem Research + Codebase Auditor | High |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:36:47.535410Z</sub>\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\n**Python 3.11:** Supported \u2014 runtime behavior verified.\\n\\n**typing_extensions Protocol:** Supported \u2014 confirmed as the base class used in `backlog_core` (`from typing_extensions import Protocol`).\\n\\n**ty (Astral) 0.0.28:** [CROSS-ANGLE] Supported \u2014 verified via `ty check` on test files. ty correctly:\\n- Catches calling sub-Protocol methods on a base Protocol type without narrowing (`error[unresolved-attribute]`)\\n- Passes when `isinstance()` narrowing precedes the sub-Protocol method call\\n- Passes when `TypeGuard` narrowing precedes the call\\n- Does NOT flag `b: GitHubCapableBackend = BeadsBackend()` as an error \u2014 because BeadsBackend structurally satisfies `GitHubCapableBackend` (has the method names). This is correct structural-typing behavior, not a ty bug.\\nSOURCE: live `ty check` on `/tmp/test_protocol_ty*.py`, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] CRITICAL FINDING \u2014 AC cannot be satisfied by structural isinstance alone:**\\n\\n`isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns **True** at runtime \u2014 even if `GitHubCapableBackend` is a sub-Protocol that adds `get_github` and `try_get_github`. This is because BeadsBackend already HAS those method names as stubs (`beads_backend.py` lines 169\u2013176):\\n\\n- `get_github`: raises `NotImplementedError(_ADR_001_NOTE)` (line 170)\\n- `try_get_github`: returns `None` (line 173)\\n\\nSince `@runtime_checkable` isinstance checks only for name presence, the structural sub-Protocol pattern alone cannot produce `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False`. The Acceptance Criteria for #2287 require a non-structural discriminator.\\n\\nSOURCE: beads_backend.py lines 169\u2013176; live runtime test confirmed `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend) == True`, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**Verified workarounds (all tested live):**\\n\\n1. **Marker method approach (recommended):** Add a unique sentinel method to `GitHubCapableBackend` that is NOT present in `BeadsBackend` \u2014 e.g., `_github_capability_marker(self) -> None`. Confirmed: `isinstance(BeadsLike(), GitHubCapableBackend) == False` when the marker is absent from `BeadsLike`. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n2. **Remove method stubs from BeadsBackend:** If `get_github` and `try_get_github` are removed from `BeadsBackend` entirely, `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), GitHubCapableBackend)` returns `False`. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n3. **TypeGuard checking a flag value:** A `TypeGuard` function checking `getattr(backend, '_is_github_capable', False)` (attribute VALUE not name presence) avoids the structural isinstance limitation entirely and is consistent with the existing capability-flag pattern. SOURCE: live runtime test, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\n- External community sources (CPython issue tracker, GitHub discussions, PyPI bug reports) not searched by either the API State or Ecosystem Research angles. If known bugs in sub-Protocol `isinstance()` behavior exist beyond the Python 3.12.0 regression, they may not be captured here. Priority: Low \u2014 the live runtime verification on Python 3.11.15 directly confirms behavior.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- API State: typing_extensions changelog not fetched (no `__version__` attribute in installed version; exact version unknown).\\n- Impact Measurement: No runtime performance benchmark for `isinstance()` on `@runtime_checkable` Protocol in Python 3.11 specifically.\\n- Impact Measurement: No benchmark comparing isinstance-guard overhead vs capability-flag check overhead for the 32 call sites.\\n- Codebase Auditor: BeadsBackend test suite (`backlog_core/tests/`) not scanned for existing isinstance tests against `BeadsBackend` \u2014 may have existing tests that encode current behavior expectations.\\n- Codebase Auditor: `GitHubBackend` and `SQLiteBackend` implementations not examined to confirm they also have `get_github`/`try_get_github` (and whether they would satisfy `GitHubCapableBackend` structurally \u2014 important for the marker method approach).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:34:08.891826Z</sub>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:31:52.945386Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: This is a defect \u2014 a design flaw in the Protocol structure that allows callers to invoke GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends at runtime with no type-system warning. The cause chain is traceable: (1) Protocol mixes generic + GitHub-specific methods, (2) BeadsBackend implements the full Protocol despite having no GitHub integration, (3) operations.py calls GitHub methods directly without type discrimination, (4) runtime NotImplementedError results with no compile-time signal. The system is broken by design and can be reproduced.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill \u2014 evidence chain from Protocol structure through BeadsBackend implementation to operations.py call sites.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Current \u2014 callers invoke GitHub-specific methods on BeadsBackend and receive runtime NotImplementedError with no type-system warning; impossible to distinguish unsupported operation from operation failure. Improved \u2014 Protocol split into generic BacklogBackend + GitHubCapableBackend sub-Protocol; BeadsBackend implements only generic Backend; operations.py narrows type to GitHubCapableBackend before invoking GitHub methods; mypy/ty catches call-site errors at compile time.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\nSOURCE: All measurements via `wc -c` / `grep -c` / `sed` on local files, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**Baseline (existing files):**\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Est. tokens (\u00f74) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `backend_protocol.py` (full file) | 38,348 | 10,957 | 9,587 |\\n| `BacklogBackend` Protocol class only (lines 156\u2013947) | 27,996 | 7,999 | 6,999 |\\n| `operations.py` | 238,065 | 68,019 | 59,516 |\\n| `beads_backend.py` | 30,084 | 8,595 | 7,521 |\\n\\n**Delta (additions):**\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Cost tier |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol + TypeGuard helper [pre-impl estimate] | ~1,265 | ~361 | Negligible |\\n\\nFormula used: tokens \u2248 chars \u00f7 3.5 (conservative). Estimate based on measured canonical example, 2026-06-18.\\n\\n**Call site inventory:**\\n\\nAll call sites currently call module-level wrappers in `operations.py` that delegate to `get_config().backend.get_github()` / `try_get_github()`. None currently guard with `isinstance()` before calling \u2014 they rely on the backend raising `NotImplementedError` or returning `None`.\\n\\n| Location | Call sites | Type |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `operations.py` \u2014 module-level wrapper defs | 2 (lines 109, 118) | wrapper definitions |\\n| `operations.py` \u2014 callers of module-level wrappers | 28 | indirect delegation |\\n| `server.py` \u2014 direct backend calls | 2 (lines 3990, 4159) | direct `backend.get_github()` |\\n| **Total** | **32** | |\\n\\n**Context window impact:** The sub-Protocol addition (~361 tokens) is Negligible \u2014 under 0.1% of a 128k context. No blocking-risk artifacts.\\n\\n**Performance:** No benchmarks found after searching `backend_protocol.py`, `operations.py`, `beads_backend.py`, and `backlog_core/` README files. The Python 3.12.0 `isinstance()` regression (~20x slower on `@runtime_checkable` Protocols) does not apply to Python 3.11 (the runtime in use).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:27:48.994185Z</sub>\\n\\n### Research \u2014 Python typing.Protocol @runtime_checkable / BacklogBackend Protocol split \u2014 2026-06-18\\n\\nResearch method: 4-angle independent fan-out (API State, Ecosystem Research, Impact Measurement, Codebase Auditor). [CROSS-ANGLE] marks findings confirmed by 2+ independent angles. No conflicts detected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-18T08:32:15.685833Z</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via /find-cause skill\\n\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. Observed failure: Callers cannot distinguish \\\"operation unsupported by this backend\\\" from \\\"operation failed\\\" when invoking GitHub-specific methods on non-GitHub backends like BeadsBackend.\\n2. Why: BeadsBackend implements the full BacklogBackend Protocol even though it has no GitHub integration; GitHub-specific methods raise NotImplementedError.\\n3. Why: The Protocol design interleaves generic backlog operations (CRUD, status mutations, serialization) with GitHub-specific methods (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2) in a single interface definition.\\n4. Why: All backend implementations must conform to the same Protocol signature. No type-system way to express \\\"this backend supports GitHub\\\" or \\\"this backend does not support GitHub.\\\"\\n5. Why: Protocol design allows operations.py to call GitHub-specific methods directly on a generic BacklogBackend type without any compile-time type check or runtime type guard.\\n\\n**Root cause**: BacklogBackend Protocol mixes generic and GitHub-specific methods in a single interface (`backend_protocol.py:156-193`). This design choice forces all backends to claim conformance to the full Protocol, even when they lack GitHub integration. Call sites in `operations.py` (lines 115, 124, 135, 150, 159, 179, 198, 207, 216) invoke GitHub methods directly on the generic `BacklogBackend` type without type discrimination, so calling these methods on BeadsBackend produces silent NotImplementedError at runtime.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: Split `backend_protocol.py` BacklogBackend Protocol into two: a generic `BacklogBackend` (CRUD, status, serialization) and a `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol extending it (GraphQL, integration branches, Projects V2 operations). Update `BeadsBackend` to implement only `BacklogBackend`. Narrow type of `repository` in operations.py call sites to `GitHubCapableBackend` before invoking GitHub methods (34 locations identified in Impact section).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Concerns\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n**Alignment rationale:**\\n\\n1. **Strengthens pluggable backend abstraction** \u2014 The development harness CLAUDE.md (line 3) establishes the core identity as a \\\"language-agnostic development process harness.\\\" Backend abstraction is the mechanism enabling that language-agnosticism. The proposed split to separate generic `BacklogBackend` from GitHub-specific `GitHubCapableBackend` directly reinforces this mission by enforcing cleaner boundaries between platform-agnostic and platform-specific operations.\\n\\n2. **Maintains composition model discipline** \u2014 CLAUDE.md Composition Model (lines 187\u2013215) and backend-providers.md (lines 104\u2013120) document that the harness owns \\\"pluggable backend abstraction\\\" with explicitly documented method groups divided between generic (CRUD, sync, serialization) and GitHub-specific (GraphQL mutations, integration branch management, Projects V2). The current monolithic Protocol violates this documented separation; splitting it restores the designed architecture.\\n\\n3. **Enables clean protocol conformance for non-GitHub backends** \u2014 backend-providers.md (lines 122\u2013130) lists four active backends: GitHub, SQLite, InMemory, and Beads. The BeadsBackend currently satisfies no Protocol boundary and masks failures via `__getattr__`. The proposed `GitHubCapableBackend` sub-Protocol allows BeadsBackend to implement only the generic `BacklogBackend` Protocol, making type-system guarantees explicit and testable via `isinstance(BeadsBackend(), BacklogBackend)`.\\n\\n4. **Addresses documented architectural debt** \u2014 Item description cites ADR-001: \\\"refactor to GitHubSpecificBackend sub-Protocol later to avoid auditing 30+ call sites.\\\" The proposal implements exactly that ADR decision. Historical direction is aligned: this refactor was explicitly deferred pending cost-benefit analysis and has now passed that gate (AI-parallelized execution makes 30 call-site changes tractable).\\n\\n5. **Follows type-system discipline established in the harness** \u2014 The harness uses Protocol-based abstraction throughout (BacklogBackend, TaskBackend, ArtifactBackend per CLAUDE.md and backend-providers.md). This proposal extends the established pattern: type-narrow at call sites before invoking capability-specific methods. The acceptance criteria (AC5: type narrowing OR runtime capability checks; AC6: ty exits 0 with no per-line suppressions) enforce this discipline at the type-checker level, consistent with the harness's commitment to observable correctness (CLAUDE.md \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact\\\").\\n\\nNo contradictions with the harness mission, design principles, or historical direction identified.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **split backlogbackend protocol to separate github-specific methods from generic backlog operations** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py::BacklogBackend` \u2014 defines the single monolithic Protocol (46 methods) that must be split into `BacklogBackend` (22 generic) + `GitHubCapableBackend` (24 GitHub-specific) sub-Protocol; `@runtime_checkable` and `Protocol` inheritance must be present on both; `__all__` must be updated to export the new name | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is the primary change surface; incorrect Protocol MRO or missing `@runtime_checkable` will silently break `isinstance()` checks across all consumers; every downstream backend, test, and CI job depends on this file's exports\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py::BeadsBackend` \u2014 currently satisfies the full `BacklogBackend` Protocol via `get_github`/`try_get_github` stubs + `__getattr__` workaround; after the split it must satisfy only `BacklogBackend` (generic) and must NOT satisfy `GitHubCapableBackend`; `__getattr__` and the stub implementations of `get_github`/`try_get_github` must be removed or the marker-method approach must be applied | Risk: HIGH | Why: the `isinstance(backend, BacklogBackend)` gate in `memory_backend.py` and `sqlite_backend.py` (lines 15, 24 respectively) plus the `assert isinstance(beads_backend, _BacklogBackend)` in `test_cross_backend.py:917` are live conformance checks; if `BeadsBackend` loses conformance to `BacklogBackend` the factory is broken; if it gains conformance to `GitHubCapableBackend` AC3 fails\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py::GitHubBackend` \u2014 must declare conformance to both `BacklogBackend` and `GitHubCapableBackend`; class signature must be updated to inherit or structurally satisfy both Protocols; `get_github()`/`try_get_github()` implementations at lines 70\u201384 are unchanged but must now be mapped to the sub-Protocol | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: structural Protocol does not require explicit inheritance but `isinstance()` verification for AC4 requires the class to expose all methods defined in both Protocols; any missing method from `GitHubCapableBackend` silently fails AC4\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py::InMemoryBackend` \u2014 currently implements `get_github`/`try_get_github` with `RuntimeError`/`None` stubs (lines 124\u2013130) to satisfy the Protocol; after the split these stubs either remain (and `InMemoryBackend` incorrectly satisfies `GitHubCapableBackend` unless the marker-method approach is used) or are removed; the `assert isinstance(backend, BacklogBackend)` at line 15 must continue to pass | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: `InMemoryBackend` is used as a test double in many tests and as a live backend for CI; incorrect Protocol satisfaction status breaks the conformance gate or causes test assertions to fail\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/sqlite_backend.py::SQLiteBackend` \u2014 same category as `InMemoryBackend`; `get_github`/`try_get_github` stubs at lines 245\u2013251 raise `RuntimeError`/return `None`; `assert isinstance(backend, BacklogBackend)` at line 24 must continue to pass; stub branch operations at lines 1088\u20131149 already raise `RuntimeError` and are GitHub-specific candidates | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: same as InMemoryBackend; additionally `SQLiteBackend` is the matrix CI backend for `test-cross-backend` and breaking its Protocol conformance fails the CI gate\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::get_github` / `::try_get_github` \u2014 module-level wrappers at lines 109/118 that delegate to `get_config().backend.get_github(...)` / `get_config().backend.try_get_github(...)`; these call GitHub-specific methods on a `BacklogBackend`-typed backend without any type narrowing; all 34 call sites (identified in Acceptance Criteria) that invoke these wrappers need either (a) a `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` narrow or (b) a `TypeGuard` function guard before the delegation | Risk: HIGH | Why: without narrowing, `ty check` will emit `unresolved-attribute` errors on `get_config().backend.get_github()` when `backend` is typed as `BacklogBackend` (which no longer has `get_github` after the split); AC6 requires zero per-line suppression; this is the highest-volume change (34 call sites)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 two direct `backend.get_github(repo)` calls at lines 3990 and 4159 that bypass the `operations.py` wrappers; these must be narrowed independently; likely require the same `isinstance(backend, GitHubCapableBackend)` guard pattern | Risk: HIGH | Why: direct calls to `backend.get_github()` on an un-narrowed `BacklogBackend` type will fail `ty` type-check after the Protocol split; these are not covered by the `operations.py` wrapper guard\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::isinstance(backend, BeadsBackend)` \u2014 line 1781 uses a concrete `BeadsBackend` isinstance check to route to `create_beads_issue_for_item`; this pattern is correct but becomes an example of the inconsistency the refactor is meant to resolve (mixing Protocol conformance with concrete type checks); likely remains valid but should be reviewed for whether a `supports_github_api: bool` flag or `GitHubCapableBackend` check would be more consistent | Risk: LOW | Why: concrete isinstance check